            ),
            // Infrastructure: application architecture
            B.q("sa-m5-47", "sa-app-architecture", "Thousands of IoT devices must publish and subscribe to messages over MQTT through a managed broker, with events also routed to Azure services. What do you recommend?", listOf("The MQTT broker in an Azure Event Grid namespace", "Azure Storage queues", "Azure Files", "Azure Batch"), setOf(0), "Event Grid namespaces include an MQTT broker. Devices publish and subscribe over MQTT, a lightweight messaging protocol, and messages can be routed on to other Azure services.",
                scenario = "Glowworm Lighting's smart bulbs talk MQTT, and its Android app controls them. The company's self-run MQTT broker crashes under load.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    1 to "Storage queues do not speak MQTT.",
                    2 to "File shares are not a messaging broker.",
                    3 to "Batch runs compute jobs.",
                ),
            ),
            B.q("sa-m5-48", "sa-app-architecture", "Whenever a resource is created or deleted in a subscription, an automation must run within seconds, without polling. What do you recommend?", listOf("An Event Grid system topic for the subscription, with a subscription that triggers a function", "Query the activity log every hour", "An Azure Policy audit", "A budget alert"), setOf(0), "Azure publishes resource events, such as create and delete, to Event Grid. A system topic for the subscription with an event subscription pushes them to a handler, such as a function, within seconds.",
                scenario = "Torrent Media wants every new resource in its Android app subscriptions to be logged in its CMDB the moment it is created.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    1 to "Hourly polling is late and wasteful.",
                    2 to "Policy audit reports compliance; it does not trigger automations.",
                    3 to "Budget alerts react to spending.",
                ),
            ),
            B.q("sa-m5-49", "sa-app-architecture", "A breaking change to an API is coming, but older Android app versions in the field must keep calling the old contract for a year. What do you recommend in API Management?", listOf("Publish the new contract as a new API version, such as v2 in the path, while keeping v1", "Change the existing API in place", "Create a revision and make it current", "Block old app versions immediately"), setOf(0), "API Management versions let you publish breaking changes side by side, such as /v1 and /v2, so old clients keep working. Revisions are for non-breaking changes to the same version.",
                scenario = "Sandpiper Bank's Android app is updated slowly by its customers. Old versions must keep working while new ones use a redesigned payments API.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    1 to "Changing in place breaks every old app at once.",
                    2 to "A revision replaces the current contract for everyone when made current, so it suits only non-breaking changes.",
                    3 to "Blocking customers' old apps would cut off people who have not updated.",
                ),
            ),
            B.q("sa-m5-50", "sa-app-architecture", "App Configuration must keep serving settings if its primary region has an outage, with replicas close to apps in other regions. What do you recommend?", listOf("Geo-replication of the App Configuration store, with replicas in the other regions", "A second store that someone copies by hand", "Hard-code the settings into each app", "Store settings in each app's local file"), setOf(0), "App Configuration geo-replication adds replicas of a store in other regions. Apps can read from the nearest replica and fail over to another if one region is unavailable.",
                scenario = "Juniper Air's Android check-in API runs in three regions and reads settings and feature flags from one App Configuration store in West Europe.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    1 to "Manual copies drift and fail when people forget.",
                    2 to "Hard-coding removes the point of central configuration.",
                    3 to "Local files are scattered and cannot be changed centrally.",
                ),
            ),
            B.q("sa-m5-51", "sa-app-architecture", "Many AKS clusters must have their workloads deployed from a Git repository automatically, with drift corrected back to what Git says. What do you recommend?", listOf("GitOps with the Flux extension on each cluster", "kubectl apply run by hand", "A spreadsheet of what runs where", "Azure Batch"), setOf(0), "With GitOps, Git holds the desired state. The Flux extension on each cluster pulls changes from the repository, applies them, and reverts changes made outside Git.",
                scenario = "Ravenglass Telecom runs 12 AKS clusters for its Android apps. Engineers make manual changes, and no two clusters match.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    1 to "Manual commands are what caused the drift.",
